- ### Comparing
+ ### Comparar

- Quantities of compatible units compare with the usual operators, and the comparison converts
- units as arithmetic does:
+ Las magnitudes de unidades compatibles se comparan con los operadores habituales, y la
+ comparación convierte las unidades igual que lo hace la aritmética:

= units as arithmetic does:
=
- ```scala fuel
+ ```scala fuel

= units as arithmetic does:
=
= ```scala fuel
- run
+ run

= units as arithmetic does:
=
= ```scala fuel
= run
- ##
+ ##

- Out.println((6*Foot < 2*Metre).inspect)
+ Out.println((6*Foot < 2*Metre).inspect)

- Out.println((9*Foot*Foot < Metre*Metre).inspect)
+ Out.println((9*Foot*Foot < Metre*Metre).inspect)

= Out.println((9*Foot*Foot < Metre*Metre).inspect)
- ```
+ ```

- Equality across different units of one dimension uses `===`, which asks whether two
- quantities measure the same amount:
+ La igualdad entre unidades distintas de una misma dimensión usa `===`, que pregunta si dos
+ magnitudes miden la misma cantidad:

= quantities measure the same amount:
=
- ```scala fuel
+ ```scala fuel

= quantities measure the same amount:
=
= ```scala fuel
- run
+ run

= quantities measure the same amount:
=
= ```scala fuel
= run
- ##
+ ##

- Out.println((Mile === 1760*Yard).inspect)
+ Out.println((Mile === 1760*Yard).inspect)

- Out.println((Mile === Inch).inspect)
+ Out.println((Mile === Inch).inspect)

= Out.println((Mile === Inch).inspect)
- ```
+ ```

- As with arithmetic, comparing incompatible dimensions does not compile.
+ Como en la aritmética, comparar dimensiones incompatibles no compila.

- Equality is exact, whether through `===` or `==`. Two quantities count as equal only when
- their underlying values agree to the last bit. A conversion which introduces a
- [rounding difference](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating-point_arithmetic) can therefore
- leave quantities which are equal on paper comparing as unequal. For converted values, `<` and
- `>`, or an explicit tolerance, are usually the better test.
+ La igualdad es exacta, ya sea mediante `===` o mediante `==`. Dos magnitudes cuentan como
+ iguales solo cuando sus valores subyacentes coinciden hasta el último bit. Por tanto, una
+ conversión que introduce una
+ [diferencia de redondeo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating-point_arithmetic) puede hacer
+ que magnitudes iguales sobre el papel se comparen como distintas. Para valores convertidos,
+ `<` y `>`, o una tolerancia explícita, suelen ser la mejor prueba.

- Plain `==` is reserved for quantities which already share a unit type, where it compares
- those underlying values directly. Two quantities of different units have no `==` between
- them. Under strict equality, with `import language.strictEquality`, such a comparison does
- not compile, which is exactly the gap that `===` fills.
+ El `==` simple se reserva para magnitudes que ya comparten un tipo de unidad, en las que
+ compara directamente esos valores subyacentes. Dos magnitudes de unidades distintas no tienen
+ `==` entre ellas. Bajo igualdad estricta, con `import language.strictEquality`, una
+ comparación así no compila, que es exactamente el hueco que llena `===`.

